                mechanism: "CO2 is a poor greenhouse gas in the 8 to 12 micron window where a cold surface does most of its radiating, which is why a thin CO2 atmosphere warms a planet so little. Perfluorocarbons absorb precisely there, and they do it with no ozone chemistry, no rainout and atmospheric lifetimes of thousands of years. A fraction of a pascal is enough to matter, because the absorption is saturated nowhere. The point is not the warming itself but the feedback it starts: a few degrees sublimes the polar CO2 caps and desorbs CO2 from the regolith, that CO2 warms it further, and the process runs on its own. Marinova's optimised C-F mixture reaches about 8 K from 0.2 Pa.",
