                order: 6,
                title: "Decide whether the magnetic field is worth building",
                mechanism: "This is the step most often assumed to be first and is in fact optional, which the measured numbers settle rather than argue. MAVEN measured the present loss of the Martian atmosphere to the solar wind directly. At that rate, stripping a full bar takes longer than the Sun has left on the main sequence. The lack of a magnetic field explains why Mars is thin today, after four billion years; it does not threaten an atmosphere placed there on any timescale a civilisation cares about. If one is wanted anyway, a 1 to 2 tesla dipole at the sunward L1 point puts the whole planet inside an artificial magnetotail, which is far easier than magnetising a core.",
