            ),
            TerraformStep(
                order: 2,
                title: "Put mass between yourself and the sky",
                mechanism: "What stops a cosmic ray is grams of material per square centimetre in the way, and broadly not what the grams are made of, which means the regolith underfoot is the shielding and it is free. Roughly 100 g per square centimetre brings a galactic-cosmic-ray dose down towards terrestrial; past that, secondary particles produced in the shield start to offset what the shield stops, so more depth buys progressively less. Lava tubes do this for nothing: the Moon and Mars both have intact ones hundreds of metres across, already roofed by tens of metres of basalt.",
                quantities: [
