            TerraformStep(
                order: 3,
                title: "Make oxygen from the water ice, which is the bedrock",
                mechanism: "The crust here is water ice, hard as granite at this temperature, and it is the oxygen supply. Electrolysis gives oxygen and hydrogen in the usual ratio, and the energy cost is the same thermodynamic floor as everywhere else. What makes this body unusual is the other half of the equation: the hydrocarbons are already lying on the ground, in lakes.",
                quantities: [
                    Quantity(label: "Oxygen for a sea-level partial pressure", value: 1304086821658067000.0, unit: "kg"),
                    Quantity(label: "Water ice to supply it", value: 734206015756299600.0, unit: "kg"),
                    Quantity(label: "Energy to electrolyse it", value: 1.932614447247501e+25, unit: "J")
                ],
                timescale: "concurrent with warming",
                maturity: .engineeringExtrapolation,
                source: "Lunine 2010, Faraday Discussions 147, 405"
            ),
            TerraformStep(
                order: 4,
                title: "Remember that the whole surface becomes fuel the moment you add oxygen",
                mechanism: "Everywhere else, the hard part is finding energy. Here the hard part is that the ground is already an energy source and the only reason it has not burned is that there is no oxidiser. Methane lakes, ethane rain and solid organic dunes are chemically a fuel depot the size of a continent. Introduce a breathable oxygen partial pressure across that and the planet becomes flammable in a way no terrestrial environment is. This is a genuine argument for sealed habitats here rather than an open atmosphere, and it is the opposite of the usual reason.",
                quantities: [
